## What happened

In .NET 11, Microsoft completed the deprecation of the **DSA** algorithm on macOS. `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on that platform.

macOS (via Apple's Security/CryptoKit frameworks) has not natively supported DSA for years. Earlier .NET versions shimmed it through OpenSSL, but .NET 11 drops that fallback — DSA is considered cryptographically weak and has been `[Obsolete]` since .NET 7.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Migrate away from DSA (preferred):**
Re-sign artifacts with ECDSA or RSA. This is the only long-term fix.

**Option B — Use BouncyCastle for legacy verification only:**

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This works cross-platform with no OS dependency on DSA support.

**Option C — Runtime guard (if you still target both .NET 10 and 11):**

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
else
{
    // fall back to BouncyCastle
}
```

**Recommendation:** Go with **Option B** — keep BouncyCastle for signature *verification* of legacy artifacts, and stop producing new DSA signatures entirely.
